# 64 GB tier, night 64-2: the directed-worker frontier at 48 GB wired (plan.md §5.1 suite 2, §5.3).
# Before: mise run vram-set 48. After: mise run vram-set 36.
# Run: mise run night-run-3 -- --part local --queue reports/2026-09-26-64gb-tier/night-64-2.queue
#
# The worker proxy: decompose (the oracle split into one subtask per file, the closest proxy for
# "directed by a stronger model") on edit-multi-mechanical r3–r6 and edit-single r3–r5, and
# retry2 (the orchestrator's check-and-retake) on edit-single r1–r2 and create-file r1–r3,
# 2 runs a rung. Base ladders run last, once per new model, as the autonomy baseline only:
# they do not rank models. optiq has night-1 base ladders, so it gets none.
#
# EDIT AFTER NIGHT 64-1: delete every line of a profile that 64-1 dropped, and switch a
# profile that only fit at 52 to its -w52 key (and run the night at vram-set 52 for it, or
# move its lines to a night of their own). Laguna runs here on the workspace server (git mlx-lm).
#
# Order is by what decides, across models, so a short night still finishes the most
# important cells for every model: decompose edit-multi, decompose edit-single, retry2
# edit-single, retry2 create-file, then the bases.
# Expected minutes scale night 1/2's optiq step times (edit-multi ~55 s a sample at base,
# decompose ~90 s; edit-single ~30–40 s; create-file ~2.5 min at base, ~4 under retry2) by
# 1.8 for the dense Qwen3.8-27B (bench-frontier MODEL_FACTOR). With all five profiles the
# queue is ~13 h: after 64-1 it will be shorter, or split it at the bases.
#
# part kind  classes               variant   runs rungs timeout-min expected-min profile
